Azerbaijan is a cluster of contradictions and contrasts, a knot of ancient historical empires. The cosmopolitan capital, Baku, has a UNESCO-protected old city surrounded by a ring of impressive architecture on the shores of the oil-rich Caspian Sea.
The Caucasus is a unique region inhabited by many peoples, with snowy mountain peaks separating the subtropical coast. Azerbaijan, until recently unknown for Europeans, is visited by about 3 million tourists annually, the overwhelming majority of whom are Russians, Georgians and residents of the Middle East.
It is home to less than 10 million people, of which more than two million live in the capital Baku, the largest city in the Caucasus. Azerbaijan is developing very rapidly, including through the exploitation of rich oil and natural gas fields.
Another advantage of this amazing state is its unique cuisine, which uses the world's most expensive spice - saffron. A trip to this country for tourists, in addition to discovering unusual works of nature and very interesting monuments, should also focus on the culinary qualities of Azerbaijani culture.
