Maybe it's better to press the stop button in the slot machine in time?
While the lazy gambler allows himself to relax during a gaming session as the "autoplay" feature does the hard work for them, casino fans' most superstitious will be entirely devoted to light fingertips or hard hits on the spin button.
Those who swear that winning slots is just about stopping the reels at the right time when you press the spin button?
Why are they trying to influence casino gaming?
In theory, this makes sense.
So, isn't it better to stop the slot machine by pressing the spin button a second time?
When the user stops the machine by himself, it reduces the "spin time" (TOS), which makes the game faster. Thus, he loses more money in a shorter period of time!
A game round takes about 10 seconds (on average) to complete a spin from start to finish. This is "TOS".
Depending on the TOS, a 30 minute session can range from a minimum of 180 spins to a maximum of 600 spins. Thus, by pressing the "Stop" button each spin, you can reduce the time of the gaming session by 3-4 times, and lose your balance in literally 10 minutes!
