Steam rival Epic Games has taken advantage of Valve's announcement to confirm that they will welcome video games that pay to play and that use NFTs.
In short, NFTs are unique digital receipts (stored on a blockchain, a kind of irreversible ledger database) that correlate with ownership of a jpg file or some other piece of digital media. NFTs are typically purchased with cryptocurrencies such as Bitcoin or Ethereum (also blockchain transactions), which can be sold for US dollars and other national currencies on exchanges.
Recent examples include the infamous Evolved Apes saga, in which a developer sold NFT on the promise that they would be included in a fighting game, but then it appears that he took the money and ran away. There are some potentially interesting game concepts that use NFT, but it's hard to say how many of them would have been a good fit on Steam even if they were allowed.
“While I respect his decision, I fundamentally believe that NFTs and blockchain gaming are the future. It is the reason why I started this journey with all of you.
“As a technology, the blockchain is nothing more than a distributed transactional database with a decentralized business model that encourages investment in hardware to expand the capacity of the database. This is useful whether a particular use of it is successful or not. '
