3 Are you sure Binance is not a scam ? : call for transparency
El exchange of Bitcoin most used in the world (mainly because of its wallet of futures), July has started very busy and with opinions for all tastes: there are those who are convinced that Binance is a scam, and there are those who believe that Binance is safe. The company led by Changpeng Zhao has had to deal with several regulatory problems both in the UK and globally, the most recent of which was an investigation by the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) of Thailand.
In April 2021, the financial regulator alemán BaFin warned that Binance, one of the world's largest cryptocurrency exchanges, is at risk of being fined for offering its digital security tracking tokens without publishing a prospectus to investors.
On June 25, the Financial Services Agency (FSA) of Japan issued a second warning against the exchange, notifying its directors that the company continues to operate in the country without being registered.
In July, the Securities and Exchange Commission Thailand (SEC) announced that it had filed a criminal complaint against the exchange for unregistered operations.
Where is its headquarters?
While the exchange platform has entities in multiple overseas locations, only now has it declared that its Cayman Islands entity is not used to carry out exchange activities - after a statement last week by the Cayman Islands Monetary Authority (CIMA), which said that the exchange was not licensed to offer crypto services.
The FCA has not only made a statement about Binance in particular, but He also sent a letter asking electronic money companies operating in the country to make clear to their clients how their money is protected..
"We remain concerned that many e-money companies do not adequately report protection differences between their services and traditional banking, in particular that FSCS protection does not apply," the FCA wrote, underscoring the importance of giving clients "a fair and prominent indication of any risk."
The FCA gave the exchanges six weeks from the publication of the letter to remind its customers how their money is protected and to make it clear that the FSCS protection does not apply to them. Since then, various cryptocurrency firms, including Coinbase, Wirex and Uphold, have issued statements to their clients.
The message also does not mention instant transfers, but only those made with a credit or debit card (regardless of whether the Binance card or any other card is used).
Although Barclays has stopped card transfers to Binance, it has assured customers that this action does not affect your ability to withdraw funds from the exchange.
“Be wary of online and social media ads that promise high returns on investments in crypto assets or related products. Always be wary if you are contacted unexpectedly, pressured to invest quickly, or promised returns that seem too good to be true. "
This warning is particularly relevant to Binance, as the platform's Binance Smart Chain has gained relevance in recent weeks due to hacks, the carpet pulls of the liquidity farm (the famous rugpulls in tokens and shitcoins) and schematics pump and dump.
With regulatory pressure mounting, and banks taking action, Binance will have to act quickly to mitigate any further damage to its platform.
