4 Digital yuan: what is it for?
4.1 What is the "managed anonymity" of the digital yuan?
The digital yuan is not a cryptocurrency: e-yuan, Digital renminbi, CNY Digital, e-CNY…: what is it?
China finalizes the launch of its own CBDC, the digital yuan. A central bank digital currency (CBDC) uses an electronic register or digital token to represent the virtual form of a fiat currency of a specific nation (or region).
The People's Bank of China (PBOC) thus aims to become the first major central bank to issue a digital currency. While its western counterparts have taken a more cautious approach (although Europe already manages the possibility of taking similar measures with a digital euro), the PBOC has conducted trials in several major cities, such as Shenzhen, Chengdu, Shanghai and Hangzhou.
Even more so for a country like China. As more and more transactions take place with a centrally controlled digital currency, the government acquires more capacity to control the economy and its inhabitants.
The alarm in Western governments is such that the threat posed by the digital yuan (which could leave China out of the reach of international financial sanctions) was a star topic of debate last month at the G7 meeting in Cornwall, England.
This is how the digital yuan looks in the application to manage its use. Photo: AP
Among the objectives that the Chinese government claims to want to achieve with the digital yuan, the following stand out:
Provide public interest digital cash while decreasing the use of physical cash. Holdings of digital renminbi will not accrue interest.
What is the "managed anonymity" of the digital yuan?
The Chinese authorities already have the ability to track payments, and hopefully this will continue. According to whitepaper,: "The e-CNY system collects less information about transactions than traditional electronic payment and does not provide information to third parties or other government bodies, unless otherwise stipulated in laws and regulations."
«In many countries there is no type of regulation: it's the absolute wild west«Says Professor Murawski, who also points out that there are no normal legal avenues if people think they have been defrauded. “That is another reason to control cryptocurrencies: to protect the consumer.
The Governing Council of the European Central Bank (ECB) has expressed this week the intention to start the research phase of a digital euro project. The president of the ECB, Christine Lagarde, has clearly shown Europe's support for the project to create a currency that, technically, would not be a cryptocurrency, but rather a digital version of the euro (in this article you can read more about the differences between digital currency, cryptocurrencies, tokens and altcoins):
The research phase will last 24 months and aims to address key design and layout questions.
This week, Fan Yifei, deputy governor of the PBOC, reiterated the concern of the government of the Asian country about the threat posed by these digital currencies developed outside the regulated financial system.
