The People's Bank of China published this week thewhite paper(or founding document) of the e-CNY or digital yuan. In it, we can explicitly read that the central bank digital currency (CBDC) will be issued by the central bank and that commercial banks simply distribute it.
China began exploring the concept of a sovereign digital currency in 2014 following the success of e-commerce platforms Alibaba, Tencent, and Baidu. It has already distributed about 200 million yuan ($ 30,7 million) in digital currency as part of pilot projects across the country, although has yet to announce an official schedule for the launch.
The first official news of the digital currency emerged in April 2020 when a screenshot of a test version developed by the Agricultural Bank of China was leaked. The first tests of the digital yuan started in May 2020 in four cities: Shenzhen, Suzhou, Chengdu and the Xiongan area near Beijing.
Digital yuan: what is it for?Whitepapere yuan
Promoting fair competition and interoperability between the different forms of digital cash is the second stated objective, (the white paper does not explicitly refer) to the dominant mobile payment platforms AliPay or WeChat Pay.
As of the end of June, Chinese citizens have opened nearly 21 million wallets and Chinese companies have launched another 3,4 million wallets. 34.500 billion RMB (5.340 billion US dollars) have been processed in 70,75 million transactions.
