This article explores the subtle differences between currencies, cryptos, altocoins, and cryptocurrency tokens, and why the term cryptocurrency or cryptocurrency is a term that is not always used correctly.
8.1 First attempts in the Netherlands
Cryptocurrencies can be extremely difficult to understand, especially since their underlying technology (the blockchain or blockchain)it is wrapped in computer language and terminology of a technical nature. This is a great barrier for many who are interested in learning more about cryptocurrencies and blockchain technology.
Let's start by understanding the definition of cryptocurrency or cryptocurrency (we will use both concepts synonymously in this article). Cryptocurrencies are digital or virtual currencies that are encrypted (secured) using cryptography.
The creation of Bitcoin marked an important milestone in the realm of digital currencies, due to its distributed and decentralized nature. The birth of BTC precipitated the expansion of a lush and more diverse ecosystem of other currencies and tokens, which are often considered cryptocurrencies in general, even though most of them do not fall under the definition of 'currency'.
It is important to note that all currencies or tokens are considered cryptocurrencies, although most currencies do not function as a currency or medium of exchange. The term cryptocurrency is a misnomer, as a currency technically represents a unit of account, a store of value, and a medium of exchange.
Most altcoins are a variant (fork) of Bitcoin, built using the original open source protocol of Bitcoin with changes to its underlying codes, thus conceiving a completely new currency with a different set of characteristics. A central concept of modifying open source codes to create new currencies is called hardforks, which is explained in more detail in this article.
Although today, it is difficult because of its high volatility, cryptocurrencies were born to be used as we use physical cash: to pay for goods and services. Instead, tokens are created for use within decentralized applications (DApps) and their networks.
All you have to do is follow a standard template on the blockchain - such as on the Ethereum or Waves platform - that allows you to create your own tokens. Using, for example, the Smart Chain of Binance (exchange, by the way, with growing problems image), anyone can create a token in minutes.
This functionality of creating your own tokens is possible thanks to the use of smart contracts: programmable computer codes that are self-executing and do not need third parties to function.
Tokens are created and distributed to the public through an Initial Coin Offering (Initial Coin Offering, ICO), which is a means of crowdfunding, through the launch of a new cryptocurrency or token to finance the development of a project. To know what a token is, you have to look at its guts.
If you are curious to see which tokens are in fashion, we recommend you visit the cryptocurrency price bible coinmarketcap.
Although there is no doubt that bitcoin has had a revolutionary impact on the cryptocurrency space (at the time of this writing, it has spawned dozens of forks and copycats, and remains the number one digital currency in the world for market cap and other metrics), is it really the first cryptocurrency? Bitcoin is considered by many investors to be the original cryptocurrency.
Around the same time, or perhaps even earlier, the American cryptographer David Chaum experimented with a different form of electronic money. He devised a token currency that could be transferred between individuals safely and privately; Again, the similarities to today's cryptocurrencies are striking.
In 1989 (yes, you read that right) Chaum founded DigiCash to put his concept into practice. Although DigiCash went bankrupt in 1998, the concepts the company proposed, as well as some of its encryption formulas and tools, played an important role in the development of later digital currencies.
Of these, perhaps the company with the greatest lasting impact on the financial world in general was PayPal (founded by Elon Musk, current CEO of Tesla). PayPal revolutionized online payments peer to peer.
