5.1 Yield farming
Without meaning to, Dorsey gave us a wonderful definition-summary of what DeFi is.
DeFi sets itself apart by expanding the use of the blockchain from the simple transfer of values to more complex financial use cases.
When you pay with a credit card for a coffee in a cafeteria, a financial institution stands between you and the business, with control of the transaction, retaining the authority to stop or pause it and record it in its private ledger. With bitcoin and other cryptos, those institutions are out of the game.
Eliminating intermediaries from all types of transactions is one of the main advantages of DeFi.
Sorare: collecting soccer player stickers has become a very expensive hobby This is because the Ethereum platform for smart contracts (o smart contracts, agreements between two parties that automatically execute transactions if certain conditions are met) offers much more flexibility than Bitcoin.
Eg Let's say a user wants their money sent to a friend next Tuesday, but only if the temperature rises above 35 degrees centigrade according to weather.com. These rules can be written in a smart contract.
These are markets to bet on the outcome of future events, such as the outcome of a soccer game or political election. The objective of the DeFi versions of the prediction markets is to offer the same functionality but without intermediaries.
DeFi loans are collateral-based, which means that in order to take out a loan, the user has to put up collateral (often ether, the token that powers Ethereum). This means that users do not give their identity or associated credit score to request a loan, which is how normal loans work, not DeFi.
The objective of the participants is obviously to earn money, although Prediction markets can sometimes better predict outcomes than conventional methods, like surveys. Centralized prediction markets with a good track record in this regard include Intrade and PredictIt.
DeFi FAQs How can I make money with DeFi?
Thanks to the token exchange and loan applications based on Ethereum, Users can generate "passive income" by lending their money and earning interest on the loans. Yield farming, described above, has the potential for even higher yields, but with greater risk.
It largely depends on who finds them useful and why. Many believe that various DeFi projects have the potential to become the next Robinhood (popular American app for investing in stocks and derivatives), attracting hordes of new users by making financial applications more inclusive and open to those who do not traditionally have access to those platforms.
Ethereum 2.0 could address scalability issues through a concept known as sharding, a way to divide the underlying database into smaller pieces that are more manageable for individual users. In addition, it remains to be seen what the DeFi network that will emerge from the promising ones is capable of.
Ethereum 2.0 It won't be the ultimate solution to all DeFi problems, but it will be a good starting point. Especially due to the huge increase in the capacity of transactions that it will present, having a favorable impact on cost and speed.
