2 China declares war on cryptocurrencies
On the afternoon of July 8, Beijing time, many users found that the Binance APP was not accessible from China and that they had to use a VPN to log in. As with other websites, this large Chinese firewall can be bypassed using a virtual private network (VPN) service.
The Chinese government has been tightening its cryptocurrency regulations since 2017 in an effort to control capital outflows. Binance, the world's largest exchange, was initially based in China (in fact its CEO, CZ, is Chinese) until he was relocated due to the 2017 cryptocurrency ban.
En June, the situation was further aggravated when China's central bank announced that the accounts of clients involved in cryptocurrency trading would be closed. The regulations have also put an end to Bitcoin mining in the country with the closure of 26 huge BTC mining farms in addition to other small businesses.
Why has China banned a cryptocurrency NGO?
Since May, the price of Bitcoin has fallen more than 42%, in part due to downward pressure from Chinese government regulations. With China's sustained campaign against the cryptocurrency likely not ending anytime soon, Bitcoin will have to find strength elsewhere to avoid sinking further.
