It is all to do. A clear example of the phenomenon is the successful Upland, a metaverse that has shown strong growth in the last three months and is now one of the top 3 blockchain dapps in terms of daily active users.
Upland was launched in San Francisco in 2019 and has recently expanded to New York City, NY and Fresno, Californiawith the planned addition of new cities coming in 2022. You don't have to be in one of these cities to play Upland - it can be played from anywhere in the world.
Upland follows the principles of an open market economy and uses blockchain technology to ensure true ownership of digital assets: once you buy a property, it is yours forever.
The Upland metaverse is made up of different types of NFTs. The 3D MapAssets They appear on the surface of the Upland map, whereas property-linked assets cannot exist without being tied to a parcel.
Dual-phase assets start out as portable items that are negotiable. But once they are attached to a property, they become tied and cannot be traded independently.
Although not all cities have their counterparts in the Upland metaverse, the team is doing everything possible to expand it. Developers are well aware that users want to own the properties equivalent to their actual residences, and while this has not been added yet, the Upland metaverse is going to be expanded shortly.
Your neighborhood will have an automatically generated collection for it once it reaches a certain rate of development if it doesn't already exist. Advances in the development index will increase the momentum of the collection.
We can passively earn UPX on our properties every 3 hours. In addition, the more Collections we complete, the more we can raise these revenues.
As we have mentioned, Upland is very serious when it comes to simulating a virtual economy similar to the one we know in real life. The currency used in Upland is called UPX (pronounced UP-EX).
In the initial phase of Upland the UPX economy will be centrally managed by the Upland team under the supervision of founder and economics expert Dirk Lueth. In the future it should become a decentralized, player-controlled economy, although more research is needed before such a major change is made (e.g.
