This is expected to be the first phase of a larger operation that will cover the whole of Europe.
And it is not just any: Binance futures in Europe have their days numbered As negative reviews, regulatory issues, and allegations of fraud and scam towards Binance only get worse. On Friday, the same day that Malaysia ordered the exchange suspend all its operations in the country, Binance has announced that it has phased out all future derivative products in Germany, Italy, and the Netherlands..
The news also comes shortly after Binance CEO CZ first opened the door to a possible resignation or name change in the senior management position. exchange Chinese.
1.1 What are futures contracts?
This week, without going any further, the European Commission made its prosecution against crypto official announcing that it will strengthen regulation around cryptocurrency-related transactions.
Futures are a type of derivatives contract to buy or sell a specific security called an underlying asset (stock, cryptocurrency, or commodity) at a future date and at a specified price. A futures contract involves a buyer and a seller, similar to an options contract.
Official statement from Binance announcing the elimination of futures contracts in Europe
Users from Germany, Italy and the Netherlands will not be able to open new futures and derivatives positions on the platform with immediate effect. Existing positions will have to be closed over the next 90 days.
Also today, the Malaysian Securities Commission (SC) has issued a public reprimand against Binance. And this is not a wake-up call: the Government has ordered him to deactivate the website and mobile applications in the country.
Malaysian laws require that all such exchanges be registered by the SC.
Binance has already had issues with the Malaysian markets regulator in the past. Along with eToro, it was included in a list of companies that cannot operate in the country in July of last year.
Earlier this week, Binance announced that it was stopping trading cryptocurrencies in sterling, euros, and Australian dollars. This came just hours after CEO Changpeng Zhao tweeted that the company was reducing the maximum leverage that users can use to trade futures contracts from 100x to 20x.
Earlier this month, the exchange announced that it was stopping selling equity-linked digital tokens, after regulators censored the use of the cryptocurrency exchange platform's use of 'stock token' offerings.
