What for months has been emerging as one of the most promising projects in the blockchain video game ecosystem that pay to play has starred in too many missteps. After successfully completing up to five NFT car pre-sales, the Crypto Cars game team faces what is ironically looming as their biggest challenge: launching the game.
(The climatic events will be available in the F2P and P2E versions and will be explained later)
As we read in the project's whitepaper:
Only cars that are in the garage will be able to farm. The cars that are in your wallet cannot be used until they are in your garage.
You can only take care of public garages, that is, paid. If you have a free garage, they can't take care of your car.
In the same way that every transaction on the Ethereum network needs gas to function, in Crypto Cars World there will be additional expenses: tools and fuel. You will need a Toolbox to fix car wear and tear and refill the gas tank.
As has happened in so many NFT games, we confirm once again that it is not necessary for the video game to exist for it to produce economic returns (thanks to the speculative buying and selling of its cryptocurrencies and NFT objects). Although we have already seen severalgameplays, Sometimes it seems that the graphical and playable aspect of Crypto Cars World is a minor detail.
