2 Official statement from Binance announcing the elimination of futures contracts in Europe
Although Binance presents the decision to close the futures contracts as a result of the continuous evolution of the crypto ecosystem, the truth is that the cancellation of the futures in Germany, Italy and the Netherlands responds to the increasing regulatory pressure that the exchange.
This is the official statement that Binance has published on Friday morning on its website in relation to the disappearance of future derivatives in half of Europe:
Effective immediately, users in these countries will not be able to open new futures or derivatives accounts. Starting at a later date to be announced in a later notice, users in these countries will have 90 days to close their open positions.
According to the commission: "Binance is illegally operating a digital asset exchange."
Binance must also stop its marketing activities and prevent Malaysian investors from accessing its Telegram group.
That Binance closes futures in Europe is news that no one should have been caught off guard. The cancellation of futures in Europe comes amidst the growing pressure on Binance from financial authorities around the world, including Great Britain, Germany, Hong Kong and Italy.
In mid-July, Binance eliminated the possibility of being able to withdraw money ofexchange in Europe by SEPA bank transfer. One week before, Binance did the same with the income via SEPA.
