4 Why is this crypto stable causing so much controversy?
HR stablecoins they can be pegged to real life currencies, such as the US dollar, the euro or the Swiss franc, or precious raw materials, such as metals. In the case of Tether, (and as you can guess from its abbreviation, USDT) it is pegged to the US dollar.
The fact that it is pegged to a real life currency gives it a huge advantage compared to other cryptocurrencies in terms of stability and value.
Tether's sending power is another reason why it attracts so much interest. USDT can be sent to any corner of the world, converted to US dollars or euros, and withdrawn just as easily as Bitcoin.
Tether: Negative Opinions and Problems with US Banks and Financial Regulators
In April 2021, Bitfinex and Tether reached an agreement with the New York Attorney General. Both companies agreed to pay 18,5 million in fines and provide, over the next two years, quarterly reports detailing Tether's US dollar reserves.
There are approximately 50.000 million Tether coins circulating in the market right now. That there really is $ 50.000 billion hidden somewhere to match is highly unlikely.
Shortly after it was announced that the Justice Department of US would investigate USDT for possible fraud, Tether Limited published on August 9, 2021 an assurance report reviewed by Moore Cayman, an auditor of investment funds and digital assets. The report provides a breakdown of the company's assets and reserves that, according to Moore Cayman, they are around 62.700 million dollars as of June 30.
