It is not at all clear if Binance is safe and reliable. The company led by Changpeng Zhao has had to deal with several regulatory problems both in the UK and globally, the most recent of which was an investigation by the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) of Thailand.
Binance, the owner of the promising future crypto BNB, does not stop accumulating negative opinions. Recently Binance has made it mandatory have all your users verify your identity.
The problems of Binance (Coinbase's main rival) in British lands are the ones that have grabbed the most headlines and the most doubts have been sown around the possibility that Binance is not to be trusted: On June 26, the Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) banned Binance from offering crypto derivatives in the UK.
The UK's is just the latest addition to a A growing list of countries that have accused the cryptocurrency exchange of not having authorization to carry out regulated activities.
In mid-May 2021, it was revealed that Binance was facing an investigation by the Internal Revenue Service of United Statesas well as the Department of Justice.
On June 26, the exchange announced that it would stop serving Ontario users after the Securities and Exchange Commission Ontario take legal action against unregistered exchanges.
Shortly after, Binance stops operating in the UK, stops allowing money to be withdrawn or transferred through transfers in the SEPA space and stop offering your futures contracts in Italy, Germany and the Netherlands.
Founded in 2017, Binance was initially based in China, but in the wake of increased regulation of cryptocurrencies, it initially moved to Japan, then Malta, before settling for a kind of no-man's-land, calling itself "Decentralized" without headquarters. Binance has not disclosed which entities it uses to carry out the exchange activities, where those entities are based, and if they are regulated there.
“Binance.com has always operated on a decentralized basis. However, we have entities incorporated under the laws of the Cayman Islands that carry out activities that are permitted by law and are not related to the operation of crypto-exchange trading activities, ”a Binance spokesperson told the specialized crypto outlet The Block on a statement, adding: "We will work with regulators to address any questions they may have."
Are you sure Binance is not a scam ? : call for transparency
In addition, the regulator asked them to clarify the difference between placing the money in an electronic money company and in a traditional bank account.
The FSCS, or Financial Services Compensation Scheme, protects an individual or business's deposits up to £ 85.000. If a licensed financial services company goes bankrupt, such as a bank, for example, the plan may pay compensation if the individual or business involved cannot do so.
This is very important: although Barclays has stopped card transfers to Binance, the bank has assured customers that this action does not affect your ability to withdraw funds from the 'exchange'.
Barclays is not the only UK bank to take a stand against cryptocurrencies: Natwest recently issued a similar block in hopes of protecting customers from scams and fraud., while HSBC has banned clients from buying MSTR shares on its trading platform due to MicroStrategy's exposure to Bitcoin. For its part, the British subsidiary of Banco Santander has also made it impossible for its clients to operate with Binance.
