MIR4 is a game designed mainly to be played on mobile phones (its interface makes this very clear). Despite the dissatisfaction of some players with the combat, the controls and, in general, the fact that it is a port to Steam of a mobile game, the number of players continues to increase a week after launch.
MIR4 allows its players to collect utility tokens called DRACOs that can be exchanged for an in-game resource called "Darksteel.".
On its website, the team rates DRACO as "the world's first currency for commercially successful online games." And it continues:
It is also specified that to convert DRACOs into real money, you must first convert them into WEMIX credits and then buy cryptocurrencies with them. It is not the simplest of the processes that we have seen in the ecosystem earn-to-play, the truth.
Utility tokens like DRACO are blockchain-based tokens that are used to finance a company, project, or product. In this case, the users who buy DRACO will in turn help the developers to finance the game.
To be clear, MIR4 was in the testing phase since last year. The company says it offers a high level of character customization, the ability to create clans, and "free loot."
DRACO coin is purchased through the WEMIX platform and is based on the value of resources derived from the game.
Free loot that allows players to strategize and compete for treasures dropped by the field boss
The game was first released in South Korea in November 2020 and ranked # XNUMX in the download charts of major Korean app markets, which provided him with a solid player base. MIR4 also received positive feedback from players around the world regarding its battle system between clans fighting for the "Valley", the main source of Darksteel.
As we write these lines, a few days after the launch of MIR4, Most of the nearly 900 Mir4 reviews on the Steam platform are negative reviews, which means that the reception of the game has been marked as "Mostly Negative" by Valve. Although the game has been poorly received, a quick check on Steamcharts shows that the MMO is rapidly building a player base.
Drakes can be turned into an in-game resource or used to purchase in-game NFTs, which players will be able to sell to each other in a market launching next month. It seems clear that this could be the reason why players stay in the game.
On the other hand, negative comments (much more numerous) criticize elements of the gameplay itself more than the cryptocurrency aspect. Many claim that the game does not have much interaction and that you often control your character on autopilot, while others think that the game is "pay to win" (pay to win) and has technical problems with audio and server
