Ripple has announced the implementation of the blockchain company's first liquidity-on-demand service (ODL) in Japan, thus laying the foundation to drive further adoption of crypto-enabled services in Asia Pacific. "XRP is the most popular cryptocurrency in Japan", according to statements to CoinDesk from the founder and CEO of cryptocurrency trading platform Liquid Global.
XRP and Japan: this is Ripple's agreement with Japan and the Philippines
SBI Remit, the largest money transfer provider in Japan and a subsidiary of Ripple's shareholder, SBI Holdings, will be in charge of launching Ripple's ODL as part of its project to incentivize remittance payments from Japan to the Philippines.
Japan (10,50%) has one of the highest remittance costs in the world: almost double the average of all G8 countries (5,92%), According to Ripple, a company that aims to help remittance services through the use of the cryptocurrency XRP to eliminate previous financing, thus freeing up capital to accelerate its own expansion.
The popularity of XRP in Japan is a well-known phenomenon. As early as January 2021, Japan's Financial Services Agency (FSA), the country's securities regulator, announced that, contrary to the US SEC, it considered XRP to be a cryptocurrency and not a security.
The news of the agreement between Ripple, the Philippines and Japan comes at a delicate moment in the endless legal battle between Ripple and the United States Securities Commission (SEC) since last year.
