Alien Worlds is a free and somewhat tedious NFT earn to play that, after several hours of play, can get you around 10 or 15 dollars a day.
Is it really that bad?
It is the least striking to see the evolution of the price of the TLM token. Although you can check it in this link to CoinMarketCap, the image that we show you below speak for itself:
After experiencing a monumental collapse shortly after the release of Alien Worlds, TLM has moved higher since June 26, when it reached a low of $ 0,066. Initially, it was difficult to overcome the horizontal zone of $ 0,26, being rejected on July 15 and creating a long upper wick (red icon).
Unlike TLM tokens, NFTs only work on the WAX blockchain, so they must be traded on the WAX markets. As if it were an Upland, more advanced players can also invest and own plots of land (in a way similar to how we will see much better in the future in games like Ember sword y My neighbor alice) on planets, earning a daily allowance of planet-generated TLM tokens and a commission from everything TLM players mine from their land.
The problem with the Alien Worlds NFTs right now is that most of them are not that attractive. If you look at the collectibles that are popular like Cryptopunks or even the famous drawings of the obscenely priced NFT rocks, you can glimpse some appeal in them.
It's pretty easy to start mining, go find a planet, and hit mine when the timer reaches zero. But after mining and wanting to go to the market to buy more mining tools to help you mine more TLMs, you have to find the website where you can buy NFT's and buy them there with a different currency (WAX) that you have to buy from a different website first.
In that case, you have to wait until the server is not busy enough, or wait for a friendly stranger to help you by betting your CPU.
Captcha's and wallet confirmation can get tedious very quickly. Every time you try to collect your earnings you have another pop-up window from your portfolio that you have to confirm, and many times it is mandatory to do those annoying captcha's that ask you to identify motorcycles, crosswalks, etc.
It's fun to see people in Discord groups promoting their land and giving people who mine on their land bonuses on TLM or NFT's. Users also make their own little community of their own land by having their own Discord channel and naming their own lands which give the game a bit more depth that helps to immerse you in Alien World's.
Alien Worlds seems to have a stable plan to move forward: partner with Binance And having future content revolve around an established cryptocurrency market looks promising for the future of gaming. Not to mention a solid roadmap that promises more features to excite gamers.
It depends on how much you value your time.
